ead out that I can't imagine  that we're going back to the amazing  intertwined world of Dark Souls 1  my biggest concern is the ability to  warp to every single bonfire I really do  agree with the argument that warping to  every bonfire reduces the tedium of the  game I get that but I also think that  this can reduce bonfires to feeling a  lot like checkpoints rather than kicking  off points into multiple areas that's  how I felt in Dark Souls 2 I feel like  every time I hit a bonfire the bonfires  before it became redundant and I have a  feeling that's going to be the same way  in Dark Souls 3 I might be alone in this  but I loved the treks between bonfires  and Dark Souls 1 and often it led me to  discovering a part of the world that I  had missed my first time through  considering the early levels of Dark  Souls 3 is so dense and intertwined I'm  worried that they're missing an  opportunity here by making bonfires feel  like checkpoints one thing I did like  surprisingly was regularly warping back  to Firelink shrine because in my opinion  it's the first game since Demon Souls to  get the hub right if you're going to  force a player back to the hub world to  level up which is in every game except  Dark Souls 1 then you have to make every  visit to the hub feel worthwhile it has  to be comforting it has to be engaging  and different now majula in Dark Souls 2  it had the right idea but presumably  because of time constraints there are  like two times I remember where new NPCs  even showed up so a lot of the time it  was just two loading screens in the way  of leveling up the hunters dream is much  the same with incredible atmosphere in  design but I did miss talking to new  characters this new filing shrine even  just at the start of the game had me  engaged I was leveling up at the fire  keeper I was buying items from the  handmaiden and giving her umbrella to  expand her wares I was doing a bunch to  my equipment at the blacksmith and most  importantly new characters constantly  appeared a dodgy guy called yeol sold me  sorceries and had his own quest line  gray rat sold items and then he sold  even rarer items when I sent him to go  steal some stuff  a man named Leonhardt encouraged me to  invade and kill people and even killing  a woman named  elana had a character named Aegon hunt  me down within the shrine a lot happened  in my short play time and I just put it  down to talking to NPCs every time I  went back to the shrine so I recommend  you do that too one of the new stats you  can level up at the shrine is called  luck and a lot of you will remember it  from demon souls it's a curious addition  because what luck does is increase enemy  drop rates and to me this is just a  blaring sign that enemy drops are going  to be way more important in this game  and the only reason I can think that  they'd put luck in is if enemies are  going to drop really interesting gear  this time around what I experienced  lines up with this because I chose the  rusted gold coins as my starting gift  and I used them when I was fighting the  Knights and my character got tons of  sweet gear and armor and you know I  can't actually think of a single enemy  that didn't drop a part of its set or  its weapon the lothric Knights dropped  their gear the fat Knight gave me his  huge halberd the workers gave me their  set and even the fat lady dropped her  hat sunlight blade who is another  uploader he got a great machete and a  flam birch from his enemies and other  uploaders found titanite know kinds of  stuff so luck is going to be important  but my only problem with luck is that  when you invest in drop rates  you're never gonna like that stat when  you're in the late game however Miyazaki  has confirmed that there is a way to  reallocate stat points in Dark Souls 3  so you don't feel that bad investing in  luck for the early game what luck is  going to do for you is give you really  good weapon variety and most of us are  coming from blood-borne which had like  15 melee weapons in the entire main game  and in the first hour of Dark Souls 3  we've already come across more than that  I do have to admit though the weapons  don't meet the depth that blood-borne  strike weapons had however weapon arts  really do help the weapons come close to  having a bit more depth webinars are  activated with l2 if you didn't know and  the type of weapon art your weapon has  is readable in the description for  example this pickaxe has one called  galvanized where you get higher stamina  recovery for a while and this curved  sword I found  has an awesome frost blade if you press  l2 as you can see here some weapon arts  are really boring admittedly and some  weapon arts are shared across multiple  weapons but they are such a welcomed  addition and I think that boss weapons  in particular are going to be really  special weapon arts use focus which is  the blue bar below your health  spells use it as well and I rarely found  myself running out of focus but that's  probably because I wasn't playing a  spellcaster  while I do think the mana bar is  fantastic I think it's way more  intuitive than spell charges are I just  can't help but feel like they're wasting  it on all the boring sorcery and miracle  choices that I've seen so far just once  in a Souls game I wish I could play  solidly as a sorcerer or a cleric  instead sorcery and miracles always seem  relegated to this position where they  compliment a melee play style they never  stand on their own and spells I've seen  so far just seem so standard you throw a  soul arrow at an enemy use soul great  sword when they get close you buff your  weapon you fumble switching between your  catalysts and your melee wouldn't it be  cool if you could run while you cast or  if you could lay down traps on the  ground to bait enemies into or maybe you  could freeze enemies to slow them down  or cast debuffs on your enemies why have  spells remained so boring and projectile  based for thr