ecked if you look at it here which is  probably from Voort stamping and  smashing and charging all over the place  this implies that he's been regularly  stopping people who attempt to leave the  castle additionally there's this giant  root that has snaked its way up the door  towards the sunlight which implies that  no one has successfully defeated Vaught  and left this place for a really long  time it's just my theories of course and  they might be wrong but telling a story  through environments is what from is  really good at and this attention to  detail is great to see all areas will  like this roots snake all over the  ground in the undead settlement the sky  is filled with crows there are lamps  hanging bodies torture devices wells  cages I could go on and on but really  it's all visible in this footage and  it's a far cry from the repeating  textures and empty rooms of Dark Souls 2  for sure and I reckon that environmental  clutter is surely one of the last things  you do in the development of a game and  the fact that it's all here is  probably a good sign that the game is  fairly complete I was playing on ps4 and  I was really impressed with the skyboxes  the draw distance the cloth physics the  texture quality and all of that so  imagine how it'll look on PC in that  glorious 60 frames a lot of this great  quality stuff is due to the fact that  it's a next-gen game like blood-borne  was and it's clear that they've taken a  lot of cues from blood-borne as well not  only is the gameplay faster and smoother  but we can already see weapons and items  that really encourage a blood-borne  paced style the pontiff's left eye ring  for example restores health on  successive hits which is like the regain  system from blood-borne and the weapon  art for the dagger has you dashing all  over the place like you would in  blood-borne so there are a few gameplay  references to blood-borne but there are  a ton of Story references to previous  Souls games characters from D